  "panel_source": "M-II (Rhythm & Groove Motor)",
  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "### Template M10: Action Simulation for Auditory Prediction (ASAP)\n\n**Core claim**: The motor system is recruited during beat perception because motor planning provides temporally precise predictions that auditory cortex alone cannot generate. Beat perception is not auditory perception followed by motor response \u2014 it is auditory-MOTOR perception from the outset.\n\n**Neural substrate**: \n- Dorsal auditory stream: Auditory cortex \u2192 parietal cortex \u2192 premotor cortex \u2192 SMA\n- This pathway provides the anatomical basis for motor involvement in auditory temporal prediction\n- SMA generates internal temporal predictions; premotor cortex generates motor plans (simulated movements at beat frequency)\n\n**Formal structure**:\n- Auditory input \u2192 BG detects periodicity \u2192 SMA generates internal beat signal \u2192 premotor cortex simulates movement at beat frequency \u2192 simulation output sent to auditory cortex as temporal prediction\n- Prediction precision: Motor-generated temporal predictions are more precise than auditory-only predictions (motor system evolved for precise timing)\n- The motor simulation IS the prediction; groove IS the phenomenology of the simulation\n\n**Evolutionary constraint**: ASAP predicts beat perception requires auditory-motor coupling that evolved for vocal learning. Predicts beat perception in vocal learners (parrots \u2713, songbirds \u2014 mixed) and absence in non-vocal-learners (dogs, cats \u2014 \u2713; sea lions \u2014 notable exception).\n\n**Maturity**: \u2605\u2605\u2605 (Theoretically elegant; supported by TMS, neuroimaging, and comparative data; some predictions not yet fully tested)\n\n**Key references**: Patel & Iversen (2014); Patel (2021); Stupacher et al. (2017)",
